<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laurens de Graaf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1653–<a href="/years/1704">1704</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Lorencillo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Laurens de Graaf was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> buccaneer who operated from French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and raided Spanish shipping and ports in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. In May 1683, with Nicholas van Hoorn and Michel de Grammont, he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, the chief port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, holding its inhabitants for ransom. He later entered French service and helped found settlements on the Gulf coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laurens-de-graaf.jpg" alt="Laurens de Graaf" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geo. S. Harris and Sons · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Graaf,_Duel_with_Van_Horn,_from_the_Pirates_of_the_Spanish_Main_series_(N19)_for_Allen_%26_Ginter_Cigarettes_MET_DP835016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
